import dailyMessage from "../../assets/work/from-the-universe/daily-message.png";
---

<CaseSection id="context" standfirst="A personal oracle for iPhone: one message a day that feels addressed to you.">
  <p>
    From the Universe is built around one personalised message each morning, with a few small moments around it during
    the day and a reflection at night. The spec is blunt about what it is not: not a quote library, not a meditation
    app, not a habit tracker.
  </p>
  <p>
    It sits between quote apps and horoscope apps: the personal feel of a horoscope and the depth of philosophy, without
    the astrology pseudoscience or the uncanny valley of an AI chatbot.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="problem" standfirst="Quote apps compete on library size; this app bets on an emotional relationship instead.">
  <p>
    The apps in this space compete on how many quotes they hold, from 3,000 to more than 22,000. The bet here is that
    the size of the library is not what brings someone back each morning. What does is the sense that the message was
    meant for them.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no user research yet: the app has not launched. This case rests on the product thinking in the spec, and
    the metrics that will test it are set out below.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="research" standfirst="A scan of the quote and horoscope apps the product competes with, and their pricing.">
  <ul>
    <li>Quote apps compete on library size, from 3,000 to more than 22,000 quotes.</li>
    <li>
      Pricing was set against the market at $6.99 a month or $49.99 a year, and justified by the real AI compute each
      premium user costs.
    </li>
    <li>
      The positioning that came out of it: the intimacy of a horoscope app with the depth of a philosophy app, without
      the pseudoscience or the chatbot feel.
    </li>
  </ul>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection
  id="decisions"
  standfirst="The journal never leaves the device, personalisation is arithmetic, and the free tier stays generous."
>
  <Decision title="The journal never leaves the device">
    <p>
      Journal entries are stored locally and never transmitted. There are no accounts, no email and no password, and no
      third-party analytics. The date of birth is never sent anywhere: only the star sign and an age bracket.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="A backend that remembers nothing">
    <p>
      Premium questions go to a serverless backend that stores nothing: no logs, no history. Because nothing leaves the
      phone, the premium pattern insight is computed on the device.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="Personalisation is arithmetic">
    <p>
      Four layers shape each message: who you are, your energy that day, what you save and reflect on, and the time of
      day. The night reflection weights the next day's content with simple scores, not machine learning, and the star
      sign is a light seasoning rather than the main flavour.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="A generous free tier">
    <p>
      Free users get a daily message, one question and three signs a day, up to ten saves and an unlimited journal. The
      same static library is the offline fallback for premium. Paywalls appear at clear limits, and free users get more
      notifications, not fewer.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="Tone as rules">
    <p>
      The voice is warm and never a fortune cookie. Words such as "journey" and "manifest" are banned, and the app never
      says "you should". The same rules feed the AI prompts and the content review.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="Sync across devices, streaks, audio" notBuilt>
    <p>
      Left out of the first version on purpose. Cross-device sync is to be built only if users ask for it, and a streak
      only if it can avoid making anyone feel guilty.
    </p>
  </Decision>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="solution" standfirst="A morning message, small moments through the day, and a night reflection that feeds the next morning.">
  <p>
    The daily loop: a morning notification opens the day's message, with a longer reading, and options to save,
    reflect or share. During the day there are three small moments: ask the universe a question, draw a random sign, or
    log your energy. At night, a short reflection and an optional journal entry feed tomorrow's message.
  </p>
  <Shot
    n={1}
    size="phone"
    src={dailyMessage}
    alt="The app's home screen in a dark purple theme: a greeting by name, the line today the universe wants you to hear, a one-line message, buttons to save, reflect and share, and a tab bar with Home, Oracle and Vault."
    caption="The daily message on the home screen."
    notes={[
      { x: 40, y: 22, text: "The message opens by name: the morning moment the whole loop is built around." },
      { x: 30, y: 47.8, text: "One message a day, changing at midnight and cached for offline use." },
      { x: 10, y: 77, text: "Save it, reflect on it in the private journal, or share it." },
      { x: 80, y: 10, text: "The crown opens premium: one of the app's clear paywall points." },
    ]}
  />
  <h3>How it was built</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>SwiftUI for iOS 17 and up, with a home screen widget, SpriteKit and Metal, and RevenueCat for subscriptions.</li>
    <li>Premium content comes from serverless functions that call Claude and keep nothing.</li>
    <li>Built in phases from the spec, each started from a written prompt in Claude Code.</li>
  </ul>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="outcome" standfirst="Functionally complete and in launch preparation; the metrics that will judge it are set.">
  <p>
    The app is functionally complete. A pre-release audit in July 2026 fixed the widget, the notification scheduler and
    the personalisation, and the remaining work is App Store preparation.
  </p>
  <Stats
    items={[
      { value: "560", label: "daily messages in the content library, with 560 oracle answers and 563 random signs", source: "CLAUDE.md:159" },
      { value: "45%", label: "of users returning daily at day 7: the north star target, not yet measured", source: "SPEC:983" },
    ]}
  />
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="learned" standfirst="Deciding where data lives shapes the features.">
  <p>
    Deciding where data lives shapes the features. Once the journal could never leave the phone, the pattern insight
    had to be computed on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Writing tone down as rules, with examples of what not to say, made it something I could check, in the prompts and
    in the content review alike.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="role" standfirst="I built it on my own: spec, content, app and backend.">
  <p>
    I designed and built From the Universe on my own: the product spec, the content, the iOS app and the backend,
    working with Claude Code.
  </p>
</CaseSection>
